# Hammock sample environment
# Copy this file to `.env` and adjust the values for your deployment.

# --- Required ---
DISCORD_TOKEN=replace-with-your-discord-token

# --- Whisper model configuration ---
# Set WHISPER_MODEL_PATH to an existing GGML/GGUF file, or comment it out to
# allow the bot to download ggml-${WHISPER_MODEL_NAME}.bin under WHISPER_MODEL_DIR.
WHISPER_MODEL_PATH=/absolute/path/to/models/ggml-base.bin
WHISPER_MODEL_DIR=models
WHISPER_MODEL_NAME=base
WHISPER_CLI_PATH=
WHISPER_LANGUAGE=

# --- Transcription runtime tuning ---
CAPTION_OUTPUT_DIR=captions
DECODE_SAMPLE_RATE=16000

# CAPTION_CHUNK_SECS is DEPRECATED and no longer affects segmentation.
# Audio used to be cut into fixed chunks of this length; utterance boundaries
# now follow speech. Setting it produces a warning at startup and nothing else.
# Use UTTERANCE_SILENCE_MS and UTTERANCE_MAX_SECS below instead.
#CAPTION_CHUNK_SECS=3.0

# --- Utterance segmentation ---
# Audio is cut where speech stops, not on a timer. These three values are the
# whole of that behavior. The bot reports them at startup.
#
# UTTERANCE_SILENCE_MS — quiet after which an utterance is closed and sent for
#   transcription. Range 100-5000.
#   This is the one worth understanding. Too low and utterances fragment
#   mid-sentence, which is what this feature exists to prevent — a 200ms setting
#   splits on an ordinary breath and word error rate rises. Too high and
#   captions lag, because the delay between someone finishing and their caption
#   appearing is roughly this value plus transcription time.
#   Below 200ms the bot warns you.
#UTTERANCE_SILENCE_MS=500
#
# UTTERANCE_MAX_SECS — longest an utterance may run before being split at the
#   quietest available point. Range 1-30.
#   Capped at 30 because the recognizer processes a fixed 30-second window per
#   request and would truncate anything longer. It is also the memory bound: 20s
#   of audio is ~640KB per speaking participant.
#UTTERANCE_MAX_SECS=20
#
# UTTERANCE_MIN_SPEECH_MS — speech shorter than this is not transcribed at all.
#   Range 0-5000; 0 disables the filter.
#   Below roughly 300ms a segment is a click, a cough, or half a syllable, and
#   transcribing it invites an invented word. Set it to 0 if you would rather
#   have fragments than lose a clipped "yes".
#UTTERANCE_MIN_SPEECH_MS=300
#
# Whether these values are right is answerable from GET /k8s/metrics:
#   utterance_length_ms.p50 around 5-10s        -> healthy
#   p50 collapsing toward the minimum           -> silence threshold too low
#   p50 pinned at UTTERANCE_MAX_SECS            -> threshold too high, or a
#                                                  participant transmits nonstop
#   total_segments_rejected_as_non_speech rising -> an open mic in a noisy room
#                                                  (working as intended)

# --- GPU acceleration ---
# GPU support is compiled in, not switched on: it is only present in the
# accelerated image variant, whose tags are prefixed `cuda-runtime-`
# (ghcr.io/mbround18/hammock:cuda-runtime-latest). The unprefixed tags
# (:latest, :sha-...) are the CPU build and have no GPU support at all.
# See docs/GPU.md for choosing a variant and granting a container GPU access.
#
# WHISPER_USE_GPU — whether to use the GPU when one is available.
#   Leave it unset and each variant does the right thing on its own: the
#   accelerated image defaults to true, the CPU image to false. Set it
#   explicitly only to override that.
#   Setting it to false on the accelerated image is a supported choice and is
#   honored silently — useful for a CPU/GPU comparison on one host.
#   Setting it to true on the CPU image warns that the image is a CPU build,
#   names the image to pull instead, and continues on CPU. A GPU that cannot be
#   used never prevents the bot from starting.
WHISPER_USE_GPU=true
#
# WHISPER_GPU_DEVICE — which GPU to use, by index, when the host has more than
#   one. Indices start at 0 and match the order `nvidia-smi -L` lists them.
#   Only meaningful on the accelerated image with WHISPER_USE_GPU=true.
#   An index that does not exist is reported along with how many devices are
#   actually present, and the bot continues on CPU rather than failing.
WHISPER_GPU_DEVICE=0
#
# The backend actually in use — and the reason, if GPU was requested and not
# delivered — is reported at startup and stays readable afterwards at
# GET /k8s/metrics under `compute_backend`.

# --- Transcription throughput ---
# TRANSCRIPTION_CONCURRENCY — how many utterances may be transcribed at once.
#   Leave it unset and a default is chosen for whichever backend is active:
#     CPU  — available cores / 4, capped at 4
#     GPU  — 2
#   The CPU default divides by four on purpose: Whisper already uses up to 4
#   threads inside a single transcription, so one worker per core would
#   oversubscribe the machine and run slower than doing less.
#   The GPU default is small because each concurrent transcription needs its
#   own decoder state — a few hundred MB for a small model, more for a large
#   one. Raising this on a big model can exhaust the device; if it does, the
#   bot says so at startup rather than failing mid-conversation.
#   Accepted range is 1 to 32. An invalid value stops the bot with a message
#   naming the value and the range — it is not silently replaced with a guess.
#
#   Whether the current value is right is answerable from GET /k8s/metrics:
#     transcription_queue_depth rising across polls  -> falling behind
#     total_utterances_discarded rising              -> losing work, raise this
#     transcription_in_flight below the limit        -> spare capacity
#TRANSCRIPTION_CONCURRENCY=4

# --- Runtime UX toggles ---
ENTRY_SOUND_PATH=resources/announce.mp3
ENTRY_SOUND_VOLUME=0.5
ALLOW_SONGBIRD_UDP_ERRORS=0

# --- Optional transcript summaries ---
OPENAPI_KEY=
OPENAPI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
INCLUDE_TRANSCRIPTS_WITH_SUMMARY=true
